    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Myelination is one of the brain’s most essential processes, occurring across the lifespan and suffering impairment in an array of neurological and neuropsychiatric diseases. Oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs) are the proliferative progenitors that maintain a stable cell reservoir and give rise to myelinating oligodendrocytes (OLs), therefore enabling this lifelong process. Through its molecular coupling with the cell cycle, the circadian system has emerged as a distinct regulator of OPC density, proliferation, and differentiation. Here we show that the effects of circadian dysregulation in OPCs extends beyond the immediate OPC population. Microglial reactivity across mice lacking circadian clock gene Bmal1 and variation in the extent of polarization suggest intricate networks of communication between distinct glial cell populations. Future research will characterize whether those results extend to astrocytes, if circadian dysregulation affects cellular-level functions like remyelination, and whether the observed effects of knocking out Bmal1 in OPCs extend to BMAL1’s central role in the circadian system. By investigating the regulation of glial cell populations and how changes in those regulatory systems may be transduced to neighboring cell types, we hope to develop a more nuanced understanding of how these systems may be therapeutically targeted.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
